WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.439
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.439 --> 00:00:09.519
This work is about changing already existing
images and videos by adding new objects to

00:00:09.519 --> 00:00:12.440
them, or editing their physical attributes.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:18.529
These editable attributes include gravity,
mass, stiffness, or we can even add new physical

00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:20.140
forces to the scene.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.330
For instance, we can change the stiffness
of the watches in this Dalí painting and

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.640
create an animation from it.

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:29.369
Physically accurate animations from paintings.

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:30.630
How cool is that?

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.470
This is approaching science fiction levels
of craziness.

00:00:34.470 --> 00:00:39.500
Animating a stationary clothesline by adding
a virtual wind effect to the scene, or bending

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:42.930
a bridge by changing its mass is also a possibility.

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.700
The first reaction I had when I've looked
at this work was "are you kidding me? you

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:49.380
can't edit a photograph!".

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:54.210
Especially that I've seen plenty of earlier
works that tried to do something similar but

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:58.800
each time the limitations were just too crippling
for real-world usage.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.020
And the other ultimate question is always
- how much user interaction does this need?

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.780
Is this trivial to use, or is it a laborious
process?

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.759
What we need to do is roughly highlight the
outline of the object that we'd like to manipulate.

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:16.329
The algorithm uses a previously published
technique to make sure that the outlines are

00:01:16.329 --> 00:01:22.060
accurately captured, and then tries to create
a 3D digital model from the selected area.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.299
We need one more step where we align the 3D
model to the image or video input.

00:01:27.299 --> 00:01:32.770
Finally, the attribute changes and edits take
place not on the video footage, but on this

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.250
3D model through a physics simulation technique.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:40.570
A truly refreshing combination of old and
new techniques with some killer applications.

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:41.749
Loving it!

00:01:41.749 --> 00:01:45.960
The biggest challenge is to make sure that
the geometry and the visual consistency of

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:48.450
the scene is preserved through these changes.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:52.689
There are plenty of details discussed in the
paper, make sure to have a look at that, a

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:55.249
link to it is available in the video description.

00:01:55.249 --> 00:02:01.530
As these 2D photo to 3D model generator algorithms
improve, so will the quality of these editing

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.439
techniques in the near future.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:07.039
Our previous episode was on this topic, make
sure to have a look at that.

00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:11.720
Also, if you would like to get more updates
on the newest and coolest works in this rapidly

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:16.540
improving field, make sure to subscribe and
click the bell icon to be notified when new

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.480
Two Minute Papers videos come up.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

